column63:
I've been recently watching Structure and Interpretation of Computer programs, where it is demonstrated that first class functions and assignment operator with nested scope are enough to implement object oriented system.<p>The simplest example given is a Counter object. Translated from Lisp to Javascript it goes like this:<p><pre><code>      function make_counter() {
        var val = 0;
        function get() {
          return val;
        }
        function inc() {
          val += 1;
        }
        return [get, inc]  
      }

      function get_count(counter) {
        return counter[0]();
      }

      function inc_count(counter) {
        counter[1]();
      }

      var counter = make_counter();
      console.log(get_count(counter).toString());
      inc_count(counter);
      inc_count(counter);
      console.log(get_count(counter).toString());
</code></pre>
This gives polymorphism (you can define make_fast_counter() that increases by 2 and still use get_count() and inc_count() with it) and encapsulation (you can not decrease the count).
